<DocsLayout
  title="OpenSourceOM security model"
  description="How passwords, optional two-factor authentication, and personal API tokens protect /v1, why the API stays on a private network, and how to report bugs."
>
  <h1>OpenSourceOM security model</h1>
  <p>
    Core assumes it runs in a network you control. The database holds a copy of your inventory,
    IAM relationships, and findings. Protect Postgres and the API the way you protect that data.
  </p>

  <h2>Personal API tokens</h2>
  <p>
    Each admin has a personal API token. The console shows the raw value once, after the account
    is created or after a rotation. Graph and ingest routes require
    <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, <code>X-API-Key: &lt;token&gt;</code>, or
    a console session cookie. That includes graph reads, findings, the rules list, blast radius,
    ingest, rules run, and Slack export. A missing or wrong credential is 401. There is no shared
    secret and no open mode.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/health</code>, <code>GET /v1/auth/session</code>,
    <code>POST /v1/auth/logout</code>, <code>POST /v1/auth/setup</code>,
    <code>POST /v1/auth/login</code>, <code>POST /v1/auth/totp</code>,
    <code>POST /v1/auth/forgot</code>, and <code>POST /v1/auth/reset</code> do not require a session.
    <code>GET /v1/admins</code>, <code>POST /v1/admins</code>,
    <code>POST /v1/admins/token</code>, and
    <code>DELETE /v1/admins/{'{id}'}</code> require a console session. A personal token cannot
    add or remove admins. The console HTML at <code>/</code> and <code>/login</code> loads
    without a session so the sign-in page can render. See <a href="/docs/console/">Console</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep the API port private until the first admin exists. Whoever can open it first can become
    that admin. Create the account with <code>om admin create</code> before you publish the
    port when you can. The CLI never sends this header. Anyone who can open a local shell as a
    user with database credentials can scan and export without a token.
  </p>
  <p>
    People use an email and password. Scripts use a personal token. Do not publish the Service
    to the internet. Put it on a private network or behind a gateway you operate.
  </p>

  <h2>Console admins</h2>
  <p>
    People sign in with an email and password. Every account is an admin. The first account is
    created from the setup form, or with <code>om admin create</code> before the port is
    reachable. <code>OM_ADMIN_EMAIL</code> pins that first address when you set it. Later admins
    receive a temporary password and choose their own at the next sign-in. When SMTP is
    configured, an admin can request a one-hour reset link. The response does not reveal
    whether the address exists. The link works once, sets a new password, and ends that
    admin's sessions. Personal API tokens stay valid. Passwords are stored
    as Argon2id hashes. Optional authenticator-app two-factor authentication is per admin.
    Sessions are httpOnly cookies. The cookie is marked <code>Secure</code> when
    <code>OM_PUBLIC_URL</code> uses <code>https</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google and GitHub are optional. A verified provider email must match an existing admin.
    OAuth client secrets and the SMTP password are deployment secrets. Password login works
    without them, including on an air-gapped console.
  </p>

  <h2>Collectors</h2>
  <p>
    Built-in collectors call read APIs. Give them a role that cannot change infrastructure.
    Plugins run with your user privileges and are not sandboxed. Review plugin binaries the way
    you review any other code that holds cloud credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>POST /v1/export/slack</code> reads the webhook from the JSON body
    (<code>{`{"webhook":"..."}`}</code>) and rejects a <code>webhook</code> query parameter so
    the URL is less likely to land in access logs. The body is still a credential. Prefer
    <code>om export findings run --format slack</code> with <code>SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL</code> in
    the environment.
  </p>

  <h2>Console script</h2>
  <p>
    The graph view loads vis-network from <code>/ui/vis-network.min.js</code> on the API. It is
    not loaded from a CDN. The session cookie is httpOnly, so page script cannot read it.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:security@opensourceom.org">security@opensourceom.org</a>. Do not open a
    public GitHub issue for a security bug. The core repository’s
    <a href="https://github.com/OpenSourceOM/core/blob/main/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a> is the
    project policy.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
